An MLP Trained Inside an Expert Advisor: A Neural Network Without Python or Weight Files
You are developing an MQL5 Expert Advisor and want to add an ML direction filter, but the “train in Python → export weights → load the file” workflow keeps introducing technical risks: normalization mismatches, missing or incorrect versions of weights, and discrepancies in feature logic between the offline environment and the terminal. As a result, the filter may behave erratically, and debugging turns into a search for differences between the two code bases.
The purpose of this article is deliberately practical: not to build a “large neural model,” but to develop a compact MLP gate that is trained on the history of the current symbol and timeframe, is periodically retrained, and operates only on closed bars. While this filter does not replace a full-fledged research ML pipeline, it solves a specific engineering problem for the Expert Advisor: removing external dependencies and making training, features, normalization, and inference part of a single .mq5 file.
Previously, the project’s MLP was trained offline in Python, and a file containing its weights was exported and loaded into the Expert Advisor. This approach worked, but it regularly caused operational problems: different scalers, different feature versions, forgotten files in MQL5\Files, and difficult troubleshooting when behavior in the tester did not match what was expected from the Python model. Here, we propose a different approach: training a small MLP directly within the Expert Advisor itself using historical data from the same instrument on which it will be applied.
The goal of this article is a single, self-contained GridSurvivor_MLP.mq5 file: on a new bar, it assembles a training dataset, generates features and labels without data leakage, trains an MLP with ReLU + softmax, logs validation diagnostics, retrains every N bars, and uses the network’s prediction as the final gate after the divergence check and the HTF filter. The readiness criterion is simple: the network is trained without external files, inference is performed strictly with shift=1, and the behavior when the model is not ready is controlled by InpMlpFailOpen.
Training in the terminal offers three important advantages for a trading robot:
No normalization mismatches.
Features are computed by the same function during both training and inference. The most insidious flaw in the previous approach — the use of different scalers in Python and MQL5 — is eliminated here at the architectural level.
Adaptation to the instrument and regime.
The network learns from actual historical data for a specific symbol and timeframe, rather than from an abstract dataset. When it is launched on GBPUSD, it recognizes the behavior of GBPUSD; when retrained every N bars, it adapts to changes in market regimes.
Self-sufficiency.
The Expert Advisor is distributed as a single file. There are no external dependencies to lose, use the wrong version of, or forget to place in MQL5\Files.
The trade-off is that training in the terminal requires CPU time. Furthermore, a small network using short-term features will not learn what a large model trained on a rich dataset in Python could learn. This is a deliberate trade-off: for an adaptive entry filter, a self-contained design, reproducibility, and ease of maintenance matter more than the highest attainable predictive performance.
What the Network Predicts and How It Is Integrated into the Gate
MLP is not a standalone trading system here. Its role is narrower and more practical: to serve as the final entry filter, answering a single question — whether local market statistics confirm the candidate direction identified by the Expert Advisor's core logic. Therefore, the entire decision-making process looks like this: an RSI divergence identifies a BUY/SELL candidate, the HTF filter checks the higher-timeframe context, and the MLP gate only allows the trade to proceed if there is sufficient alignment between the network’s forecast and the candidate direction.
The network is a two-class classifier: "down" and "up." The input is a feature vector describing the market state on a closed bar; the softmax output consists of two probabilities — P(down) and P(up) — for a horizon of InpMlpHorizon bars ahead. Their difference, P(up) − P(down), represents the network's directional confidence: a positive value indicates an expectation of an increase, while a negative value indicates an expectation of a decrease.
The relationship "label → horizon → gate threshold" is fundamental. The label teaches the network to distinguish which way the price has moved after InpMlpHorizon bars, while InpMlpThreshold sets the minimum margin by which one class must lead the other. If the difference in probabilities is small, the model does not provide a strong enough recommendation, and it is better to reject the candidate.
The RSI divergence continues to generate a candidate with a direction of dir (+1 for BUY, −1 for SELL). The MLP gate accepts a candidate only if the network
agrees
with that direction with sufficient confidence:
bool
ok = (dir == 1) ? (conf >=  InpMlpThreshold)
                   : (conf <= -InpMlpThreshold);
A BUY candidate passes when the network predicts a rise with confidence at or above the threshold; a SELL candidate passes when it predicts a fall. If the divergence points upward but the network confidently predicts a decline, the signal is rejected. This provides dual confirmation: the formal pattern and the learned assessment of direction must agree. The order of the filters remains the same: divergence → HTF filter → MLP. MLP is listed last because it is more expensive than the other checks and should only be triggered for a candidate that has already been found, rather than for every intermediate condition.
Please note: The candidate direction (dir)
is not included
in the features. The network evaluates the market impartially, predicting its own direction, while alignment with dir is checked externally, in the gate. This is fairer than feeding dir as an input: otherwise, the network could learn simply to repeat the candidate direction.
