A Network That Learns: The CMlpNet Class
Unlike the inference engine from the previous version, this network is capable of learning. Architecture: input → one hidden layer with ReLU → softmax output for two classes. Weights are stored as flat arrays in row-major order: W1[j·nIn + i] is the weight of the connection from input i to neuron j in the hidden layer, and W2[k·nHid + j] is the weight of the connection from neuron j to output k. He initialization is used with a standard deviation of sqrt(2/n_in), which is appropriate for ReLU.
Forward pass: the hidden layer computes weighted sums and applies ReLU, the output layer produces logits, and softmax converts them into probabilities.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Forward pass: input x → class probabilities probs.               |
//| Saves z1 and h for backpropagation.                              |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
Forward(
const
double
&x[],
double
&probs[])
  {
for
(
int
j=0;j<m_nHid;j++)
     {
double
z=m_b1[j];
for
(
int
i=0;i<m_nIn;i++) z+=m_W1[j*m_nIn+i]*x[i];
      m_z1[j]=z; m_h[j]=ReLU(z);
     }
   ArrayResize(probs,m_nOut);
for
(
int
k=0;k<m_nOut;k++)
     {
double
z=m_b2[k];
for
(
int
j=0;j<m_nHid;j++) z+=m_W2[k*m_nHid+j]*m_h[j];
      probs[k]=z;
     }
   Softmax(probs,m_nOut);
  }
Training — one SGD step: forward pass, followed by backpropagation of the cross-entropy gradient. For the softmax + cross-entropy pair, the output gradient simplifies to probs[k] − target[k], which is used below. Next, the gradient is passed backward through the hidden layer's ReLU, and the weights are updated according to the rule W ← W − lr · grad.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| One SGD step: forward pass + backpropagation + weight update.    |
//| Calculates the gradients and updates W1, b1, W2, b2.             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
Step(
const
double
&x[],
int
label)
  {
double
probs[]; Forward(x,probs);
//--- output gradient (softmax + cross-entropy)
double
dOut[]; ArrayResize(dOut,m_nOut);
for
(
int
k=0;k<m_nOut;k++) dOut[k]=probs[k]-(k==label?1.0:0.0);
//--- hidden layer gradient (via ReLU)
double
dH[]; ArrayResize(dH,m_nHid);
for
(
int
j=0;j<m_nHid;j++)
     {
double
g=0.0;
for
(
int
k=0;k<m_nOut;k++) g+=dOut[k]*m_W2[k*m_nHid+j];
      dH[j]=g*dReLU(m_z1[j]);
     }
//--- output layer update
for
(
int
k=0;k<m_nOut;k++)
     {
for
(
int
j=0;j<m_nHid;j++) m_W2[k*m_nHid+j]-=m_lr*dOut[k]*m_h[j];
      m_b2[k]-=m_lr*dOut[k];
     }
//--- hidden layer update
for
(
int
j=0;j<m_nHid;j++)
     {
for
(
int
i=0;i<m_nIn;i++) m_W1[j*m_nIn+i]-=m_lr*dH[j]*x[i];
      m_b1[j]-=m_lr*dH[j];
     }
  }
A full training cycle runs for a specified number of epochs, shuffling the order of the examples in each epoch using the Fisher–Yates shuffle. Shuffling is important: without it, SGD "drifts" through the examples in chronological order and converges less effectively.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Full training cycle on the X/Y dataset.                          |
//| Shuffles examples in each epoch for stable convergence.          |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
Train(
const
double
&X[],
const
int
&Y[],
int
n,
int
nIn,
int
epochs)
  {
   Reset();
int
idx[]; ArrayResize(idx,n);
for
(
int
i=0;i<n;i++) idx[i]=i;
for
(
int
ep=0;ep<epochs;ep++)
     {
//--- Fisher–Yates shuffle
for
(
int
i=n-1;i>0;i--)
        {
int
j=MathRand()%(i+1);
int
t=idx[i]; idx[i]=idx[j]; idx[j]=t; }
for
(
int
i=0;i<n;i++)
        {
double
xi[]; ArrayResize(xi,nIn);
int
row=idx[i]*nIn;
for
(
int
f=0;f<nIn;f++) xi[f]=X[row+f];
         Step(xi,Y[idx[i]]);
        }
     }
   m_ready=
true
;
  }
The correctness of the algorithm has been verified by independently replicating the code. On a synthetic task with a relationship known to be learnable, the network achieves approximately 96% accuracy on the training set. This confirms that the row-major layout of the weights W1[j·nIn+i] / W2[k·nHid+j] is consistent between the forward and backward passes.
Dataset Collection and Training on Historical Data
The TrainModel function builds a training set by iterating through the history from the most recent closed bars to the oldest, calculating the features and label for each bar. Next comes a simple quality assessment: the most recent 20% of bars are set aside for validation, a temporary network is trained on the older 80%, and accuracy is calculated on the held-out bars. This diagnostic result is written to the log and helps determine whether the network provides a useful signal. After evaluation, the final network is trained on the entire sample.
