This differs from the Markov indicator. There, the features were constructed from a transition matrix estimated from the data, so when splitting the data into train and validation sets, the matrix had to be built using only the train bars; otherwise, transitions from the validation period would “leak” into the features. In our case, the features are purely local — they are the RSI, ATR, and EMA readings on a single bar and do not depend on any statistics fitted to the dataset. Therefore, there is no data leakage through a shared object, and validation remains unbiased without any additional workarounds. The network still does not see the labels of the validation bars — it is trained only on the training rows.
Integration into the Expert Advisor
The minimum practical integration requires four steps: add the MLP parameters, create an EMA handle for the features, run TrainModel on a new bar, and call MlpAccepts(dir) after the HTF filter in CheckSignal. Everything else consists of network implementation and diagnostic details.
Importantly, the MLP must not directly change the existing trading logic. It only accepts or rejects an already generated candidate. This simplifies debugging: if a candidate appears but no trade is opened, you can separately check the divergence, the HTF filter, network readiness, the conf value, and the InpMlpThreshold threshold in the logs.
